{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-noop-commit.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 34 turned the client-completion investigation into a new, sharper lead: a legal NOOP commit can terminate the replica that owns the instance.\\n\\nHow the lead appeared. While working out what happens to a client proposal after recovery commits a NOOP for another replica's instance, I checked where that NOOP commit goes. handleCommit has a branch for a commit on the replica's own row carrying a single NONE command: it re-proposes that instance's pending client proposals, and the branch dereferences inst.lb.clientProposals without checking inst.lb. Records created by message handlers (handlePreAccept, handleAccept, handlePrepare, handleCommit itself) never get leader bookkeeping, so a replica that owns an instance it did not propose in this process lifetime — for example after a restart, with a recovery-led PreAccept having recreated the record — dereferences nil.\\n\\nHarness and observation. The new in-package harness creates that record with the real handlePreAccept (ballot 3, one PUT command) and then delivers a well-formed NOOP Commit (ballot 4) to the real handleCommit, wrapping only the delivery in recover() so the observation is reported instead of killing the test process. The local construction run gives record_from_message with bookkeeping_nil true, then handle_commit_result with panicked true, panic_value \\\"runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\\\" and no_effect true. The handlers are unmodified; the run loop's lack of a recover is noted as an uncertainty and a semantic difference, since the panic is what the harness reports rather than modelled process death.\\n\\nObligation (new claim C4-noop-commit-without-bookkeeping, bounded liveness, priority 3). A legal NOOP commit for an instance the replica owns must be processed without dereferencing absent bookkeeping and without terminating the replica; the check asserts panicked == false on the commit-result event, with the message-created record as a prerequisite alias and instance identity correlation. The claim's grounding derives the duty from the handler's own branch and the record-creation paths, its scope keeps the client reaction and the NOOP history out, and its alternative reading (that such a record can never be the owner's) is named as what the message-created record contradicts. The plan records the substituted dispatcher, the direct handler calls and one instance.\\n\\nState. Audit spec version 6; the three confirmed claims are untouched; the accept-status candidate stays paused; the client-completion candidate remains active with its remaining unknowns, and the open scope issue on the superseded C1 artifact stays visible as bookkeeping debt.\"}"}}
